      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate document carefully.\n\n**Certificate details:**\n- Number: R49/2006-DE1-12.05\n- Issuing Authority: Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt (PTB)\n- Address: Bundesallee 100, 38116 Braunschweig\n- Person responsible: Dr. Gudrun Wendt (also Dr. G.Wendt, Head of Department) and Dr. R. Schwartz, Head of Division (CIML Member)\n- Member State: Germany\n- Date issued: signatures dated 06.09.2012 and 07.09.2012. The certificate date — likely 2012-09-06 or 2012-09-07. Hmm. The certificate number contains \"12.05\" suggesting 2012. The date_issued — I'll use 2012-09-07 (latest signature date) or maybe 06.09.2012. Typically the certificate date is when the issuing authority signs. Let me use \"2012-09-06\" — actually both are signed. The issuing authority signed 06.09.2012, CIML member 07.09.2012. I'll go with 2012-09-06 for issuing authority. Hmm, ambiguous. Maybe include both? The schema has date_issued single field. I'll use \"2012-09-06\".\n\nActually, let me think. The certificate number is R49/2006-DE1-12.05. PTB certificates often have issue date. The signing dates: 06.09.2012 (Head of Department) and 07.09.2012 (Head of Division). I'll use 2012-09-06 as date_issued.\n\n- Page total: 4 pages\n- Project number: \"R3-0033\" appears on page 1 — that could be PTB's project/reference number. Include as project_number: \"R3-0033\".\n- Scheme: R49 is a \"measuring system\" type recommendation... Actually OIML-CS scheme A vs B. Certificates of conformity under the OIML Certification Scheme — R49 water meters. Scheme A is typically for... Actually, in OIML-CS, Scheme A certificates are issued without test reports review? Hmm. Historically OIML certificates are \"Scheme A\". R 49 certificates — the standard OIML certificate system. The rules say scheme (A|B). Most legacy OIML certificates are Scheme A. I'll use \"A\".\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- R 49-1 (Edition 2006), R 49-2 (Edition 2006), R 49-3 (Edition 2006)\n- Canonical id: \"R49\", edition: 2006. There are three parts. The schema allows one id/edition. I'll put id \"R49\", edition 2006. Maybe note parts in... The schema doesn't have parts. I'll just use R49 edition 2006. Accuracy classes: for water meters R49, accuracy class 2 (±2%...±5%). The table says Accuracy class ±2% (Q2≤Q≤Q4), ±5% (Q1≤Q<Q2) for cold; ±3%/±5% for hot. R49 accuracy classes are 1 and 2 (or 2 and 3 for hot?). Actually R49 defines accuracy class 1 and 2. Class 2: ±5% at Q1≤Q<Q2, ±2% at Q2≤Q≤Q4. Class 1: ±3%, ±1%. Here cold water: ±2%/±5% = class 2. Hot: ±3%/±5% — hmm that's a mixed. Actually for combined water meters? The ±3%/±5% is class 3? No... In R49, temperature classes T30/T50/T70/T90. Hot water meters: class 2 has ±3% in upper zone and ±5% lower zone for T50-T90? Let me recall: OIML R49-1 accuracy classes: Class 1: (Q2≤Q≤Q4): ±1%, (Q1≤Q<Q2): ±3%. Class 2: ±2% and ±5%. For hot water (T50-T90) the maximum permissible errors may differ... Actually ISO 4064: class 2 hot water: ±2% upper. Hmm, but this cert says ±3%. That's a specific MPE. I won't over-derive. accuracy_classes: could be [\"2\"]? Given the doc lists \"±2%/±5%\" and \"±3%/±5%\", these correspond to accuracy class 2 for cold and... For safety, I could set accuracy_classes: [\"2\"] since R80 (Q3/Q1 = 80 = R80 mentioned on dials). Hmm, the dials say \"R80\". The Q3/Q1 ratios are 25..80, so R25 to R80.\n\nActually maybe just leave accuracy_classes out or include [\"2\", \"3\"]? Hmm. Let me not fabricate. The certificate lists \"Accuracy class: ±2%.../±5%...\" and \"±3%.../±5%...\". In ISO 4064/OIML R49, water meters have accuracy class 1 or 2. But the values here for hot water match \"class 3\"? There's no class 3. Actually for combined meters the second is... Let me check ISO 4064-1:2014: Accuracy class 1: ±3% (Q1≤Q<Q2), ±1% (Q2≤Q≤Q4). Class 2: ±5%, ±2%. For hot water meters above 30°C: class 2: ±3% within Q2-Q4? Yes! ISO 4064 allows for hot water meters in temperature range above 30°C: the upper zone error is ±3% for class 2? Hmm, actually I recall \"For water meters of accuracy class 2 intended for measuring hot water, the MPE in the upper temperature zone is ±3%\". Yes that's right — ISO 4064-1 Table 3: for combined... I believe the certificate is class 2 across the board. I'll put accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]. Hmm, but risk of fabrication. The table doesn't literally say \"accuracy class 2\". It says \"Accuracy class: ±2% ... ±5% ...\". Safer to omit accuracy_classes? The schema field exists but rule 8 says don't fabricate. I could include it derived... I think it's safer to omit or include [\"2\"]? I'll omit it to be safe. Actually, hmm. Let me include it as [\"2\"]? No — rule 8: don't fabricate. Omit.\n\n**Test reports:**\nThe big table on page 2 lists test report identifiers and page counts:\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 41 pages (cold)\n- ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 53 pages (warm/hot)\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 41 pages; ETW Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 53\n- ETK Q3-4 V4 80 — 61; ETW Q3-4 V4 80 — 89\n- ETK Q3-4 V3 115 — 61; ETW Q3-4 V3 115 — 89\n- ETK Q3-1,6 V1 110 — 40; ETW Q3-1,6 V1 110 — 52\n- ETK Q3-4 V3 CC 115 — 53; ETW Q3-4 V3 CC 115 — 77\n- ETK Q3-4 V4 130 — 61; ETW Q3-4 V4 130 — 89\n- ETK Q3-4 V3 8R 115 — 61; ETW Q3-4 V3 8R 115 — 89\n- ETK Q3-4 V3 KU 130 — 61; ETW Q3-4 V3 KU 130 — 89\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V3 110 — 161; ETW Q3-2,5 V3 110 — 227\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V4 80 — 145; ETW Q3-2,5 V4 80 — 245\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 161; ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 277\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V3 80 — 145; ETW Q3-2,5 V3 80 — 245\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V3 CC 110 — 57; ETW Q3-2,5 V3 CC 110 — 85\n- ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 59; ETW Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 87\n\nThese are test reports, role \"type_evaluation_report\" or \"test_report\". The certificate says \"associated Test Reports\". I'll use role \"test_report\" for each. Ids are the ETK/ETW codes. ETK = German for cold (Kaltwasser) test report; ETW = warm water test report.\n\n**Applicant:**\nZenner International GmbH & Co. KG, Römerstadt 4, 66121 Saarbrücken.\n\n**Manufacturers:** six:\n1. ZENNER International GmbH & Co KG, Mittelstraße 42, 09619 Mulda, GERMANY\n2. ZENNER International GmbH & Co KG, Römerstadt 4, 66121 Saarbrücken, GERMANY\n3. ZENNER do Brasil Instrumentos de Medicao Ltda., Rua Bartolomeu de Gusmao 2.444, Canudos – Novo Hamburgo RS, CEP: 93546-000, BRAZIL\n4. ZENNER Coma JVC. Construction Machinery Company, 125D Minh Khai, Q Hai Ba Trung, Hanoi, VIETNAM\n5. ZENNER Aquamet India Pyt Ltd., 39-B, HSIDC Industrial Estate, Sec. 31, Faridabad – 121003, INDIA\n6. ZENNER Meters Ltd., 15 Dongxing Road, Songjiang Industrial Zone, 201613 Shanghai, CHINA\n\n**Certified type:**\n- Category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"Minomess A\", \"Minomess B\"]\n- module_designation: null / \"Not applicable\"? It's a mechanical water meter — module designation not applicable. I'd say null or omit. Schema: module_designation \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll put null.\n- description: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water. Single jet meter with mechanical register.\"\n\n**Model family:**\nThe certificate covers Minomess A and Minomess B, each with multiple Q3 values (1.6, 2.5, 4), body lengths, DN, indicating device variations, orientations. This is complex.\n\nModels per type:\nMinomess A:\n- Table 1: Q3=1.6, Q4=2, Q2/Q1=1.6, body length 80 or ≥110, DN 15, indicating device Variation 1/2, Q3/Q1 H: 25/31.5/40/50/63/80; V: 25; connection types differ by length.\n- Table 2: Q3=2.5, Q4=3.125, Q2/Q1=1.6, body lengths 80/≥110 (DN15, Var 1/2) and 80/≥110 (DN15, Var 3/4). Actually table 2 has 4 columns: first two for Variation 1/2 (DN 15 and DN 20), next two Variation 3/4 (DN 15). Let me re-read:\n\nTable 2:\n- Q3 = 2.5, Q4 = 3.125, Q2/Q1 = 1.6\n- Body length: 80, ≥110, 80, ≥110 (4 columns)\n- DN: DN 15, DN 20, DN 15 (colspan 2)\n- Indicating device: Variation 1/2 (cols 1-2), Variation 3/4 (cols 3-4)\n- Q3/Q1 Orientation H: \"25/31.5/40/50/63/80\" (cols 1-2), \"25/31.5/40\" (col 3), \"25/31.5/40/50/63/80\" (col 4)\n- Orientation V: 25 (cols1-2), \"25/31.5/40\" (cols 3-4)\n- Connection type: col1: G ¾ B, M22x1.5(1)2); col2: G 1 B; col3: G ¾ B, M22x1.5(1)2); col4: G ¾ B, G ⅞ B, G 1 B(1), M22x1.5(1)2)\n\nSo Minomess A Q3=2.5 has variants: (Var1/2, DN15, 80mm), (Var1/2, DN20, ≥110mm), (Var3/4, DN15, 80mm), (Var3/4, DN15, ≥110mm).\n\n- Table 3: Q3=4, Q4=5, Q2/Q1=1.6, body length 80 or ≥115, DN 20, Indicating device Var 3/4, Orientation H: 25/31.5/40 (80mm) or 25/31.5/40/50/63/80 (≥115); V: 25/31.5/40; connection: M22x1.5 (80mm), G 1 B (≥115).\n\nMinomess B (table 4):\n- Q3=1.6 (Q4=2.0, DN15) and Q3=2.5 (Q4=3.125, DN20)\n- Q2/Q1=1.6\n- Body length 80/≥110 for each\n- Indicating device Var 1/2\n- Q3/Q1 H: 25/31.5/40/50/63/80; V: 25\n- Connection: Q3=1.6@80: G ¾ B; @≥110: G ¾ B, RP ½ B, RP ¾ B; Q3=2.5@80: G ¾ B; @≥110: G 1 B, RP ¾ B\n\nCommon characteristics:\n- Temperature range: 0.1 °C to 30 °C (cold) and 30 °C to 90 °C (hot) — \"0,1 °C bis 30 °C\" / \"30 °C bis 90 °C\". Note \"bis\" = German \"to\". So cold: 0.1–30°C, hot: 30–90°C.\n- Accuracy class: ±2% (Q2≤Q≤Q4), ±5% (Q1≤Q<Q2) for cold; ±3%/±5% for hot.\n- Temperature classes: T30/T50/T70/T90\n- Pressure range: 0.3 bar (0.03 MPa) to 16 bar (1.6 MPa)\n- Pressure loss class: 0.63 bar (0.063 MPa)\n- Environmental class: B, C\n\n**Matrix tables:** The type-detail tables are complex multi-dimensional. I should preserve them in matrix_tables and populate model_family.models and model_level characteristics.\n\nHow to structure models? Each model could be identified by combination: type (A/B), Q3, DN, body length, indicating device variation. That would be many models. Alternatively, models = \"Minomess A\" and \"Minomess B\" and use model_level for q3, q4, and config_level for body length axes.\n\nGiven the complexity, a reasonable approach:\n- model_family.family_name: \"Minomess\"\n- models: list each variant... That could explode. Perhaps models = [{model_id: \"Minomess A\"}, {model_id: \"Minomess B\"}].\n\nThen model_level characteristics:\n- q3: Minomess A: 1.6, 2.5, 4; Minomess B: 1.6, 2.5\n- q4: A: 2, 3.125, 5; B: 2.0, 3.125\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 both\n- nominal_diameter: A: DN15, DN20; B: 15, 20\n\nBut the values differ per config. Better: include matrix_tables preserving the four tables, and model_level with the axis-based config.\n\nActually, let me define models more granularly. The certificate's tables define variants by (Q3, DN, body length, indicating device variation). Let me enumerate Minomess A variants:\n\nA1: Q3=1.6, DN15, body 80, Var 1/2, Q3/Q1 H 25–80, V 25, conn G ¾ B or M22x1.5\nA2: Q3=1.6, DN15, body ≥110, Var 1/2, conn G ¾ B, G ⅞ B, G 1 B, M22x1.5\nA3: Q3=2.5, DN15, body 80, Var 1/2\nA4: Q3=2.5, DN20, body ≥110, Var 1/2, conn G 1 B\nA5: Q3=2.5, DN15, body 80, Var 3/4\nA6: Q3=2.5, DN15, body ≥110, Var 3/4\nA7: Q3=4, DN20, body 80, Var 3/4, conn M22x1.5\nA8: Q3=4, DN20, body ≥115, Var 3/4, conn G 1 B\n\nMinomess B:\nB1: Q3=1.6, DN15, body 80, Var 1/2, conn G ¾ B\nB2: Q3=1.6, DN15, body ≥110, Var 1/2, conn G ¾ B, RP ½ B, RP ¾ B\nB3: Q3=2.5, DN20, body 80, Var 1/2, conn G ¾ B\nB4: Q3=2.5, DN20, body ≥110, Var 1/2, conn G 1 B, RP ¾ B\n\nThis is getting complicated. Given the layered schema, I think a pragmatic approach:\n\n- model_family.models: entries like {\"model_id\": \"Minomess A Q3=1.6\", ...} — maybe keep at (type, Q3) granularity: \"Minomess A Q3=1.6\", \"Minomess A Q3=2.5\", \"Minomess A Q3=4\", \"Minomess B Q3=1.6\", \"Minomess B Q3=2.5\". 5 models. Attributes: q3, q4, q2_over_q1, dn... DN varies within model (A Q3=2.5 has DN15 Var1/2 and DN20 ≥110 Var1/2, plus DN15 Var3/4). Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, use config_level for the body-length axis.\n\nGiven rules and pragmatism, I'll do:\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"Minomess\"\n- models: five entries with model_id like \"Minomess A Q3=1.6\" and attributes capturing DN set, etc.\n\nActually attributes{} per model could hold: q3, q4, dn, indicating_device_variations, orientation ratios. But body length creates two sub-variants within each. I could handle body length via config_level with axis \"body_length_mm\" having conditions \"80\" and \"≥110\".\n\nLet me think about what's most useful and faithful:\n\nmatrix_tables: preserve the 4 type-detail tables + the common characteristics table + the temperature/accuracy table.\n\n1. matrix_tables[0]: \"Minomess A — Q3 = 1.6 m³/h\" with columns: q3_m3_h, q4_m3_h, q2_over_q1, body_length_mm, nominal_diameter, indicating_device, q3_over_q1_orientation_h, q3_over_q1_orientation_v, connection_type. Rows for the two body-length configs.\n\n2. matrix_tables[1]: \"Minomess A — Q3 = 2.5 m³/h\" — 4 columns.\n\n3. matrix_tables[2]: \"Minomess A — Q3 = 4 m³/h\" — 2 columns.\n\n4. matrix_tables[3]: \"Minomess B — Q3 = 1.6 / 2.5 m³/h\" — 4 columns.\n\n5. matrix_tables[4]: \"Common characteristics\" — temperature range, accuracy class, temperature classes, pressure range, pressure loss class, environmental class.\n\nThen characteristics.model_level: attribute × model matrix for the 5 models (type+Q3 granularity):\n- q3: values per model\n- q4: per model\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 all\n- nominal_diameter: per model (may be a set — as string)\n- Hmm DN for \"Minomess A Q3=2.5\" is DN15 (Var1/2 @80, @≥110) and DN20 (@≥110 Var1/2) and DN15 (Var3/4). So value would be \"DN 15 / DN 20\". That's messy but honest.\n\nAlternatively make models at (type, Q3, DN, variation) granularity. That's 8 A + 4 B = 12 models. Then model_level attributes are clean:\n\nFor A:\n- MA-1.6-DN15 (Var 1/2): q3 1.6, q4 2, dn 15, body 80 & ≥110 (two variants differ by body length + connections). Ugh, body length still varies.\n\nHonestly, body length is an orthogonal axis. Let me use config_level for body length:\nconfig_level[]: attribute \"body_length_mm\" axis \"meter_body_length\" with conditions... Actually config_level values: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. E.g. attribute \"connection_type\", axis \"q3_and_body_length\", conditions...\n\nHmm, I think the cleanest faithful representation:\n\n- models: 12 entries? Or keep models simple (5) and use matrix_tables for full detail.\n\nGiven rule 4 says detect matrix and populate models + model_level, and the tables are inherently multi-dimensional, I'll:\n\n1. Put full tables in matrix_tables (preserving everything).\n2. model_family.models: 5 models at type+Q3 granularity: \"Minomess A Q3=1.6\", \"Minomess A Q3=2.5\", \"Minomess A Q3=4\", \"Minomess B Q3=1.6\", \"Minomess B Q3=2.5\". Each with attributes: {\"q3\": \"1.6 m³/h\", ...} — attributes are free-form.\n3. characteristics.model_level: q3, q4, q2_over_q1 per model.\n4. characteristics.config_level: \n   - attribute \"q3_over_q1_ratio\", axis \"orientation\": conditions \"H\" → {min:25, max:80} set... hmm, ratios are lists: 25/31.5/40/50/63/80. Lists of values — the rule about lists applies to model matrices, but here it's an allowed-ratio list. The R80 dial says \"R80\". The ratio values are a set of possible metering ratios. I could express as value list? The schema's structured value allows number|string|range. A list like \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\" as string \"25/31.5/40/50/63/80\"? Or since it's the R-ratio range: {min:25, max:80}? The individual values matter. I'll keep as string \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\" for attribute \"q3_over_q1_ratio\" — but that violates \"numbers not strings\"? The rule says lists of values are model matrices, not list-as-value. But these aren't model variants; they're selectable ratios. Hmm.\n\nAlternative: attribute \"q3_over_q1_ratio_min\" = 25, \"q3_over_q1_ratio_max\" = 80? That fabricates structure. I'll keep the ratio set as a string value: \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\". That's honest. Footnote: dial shows R80 meaning max ratio 80.\n\nLet me now design config_level for orientation:\n- attribute: \"q3_over_q1_ratio\", axis: \"orientation\", values: [{condition: \"H (horizontal)\", value: \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\"}, {condition: \"V (vertical)\", value: \"25\"}] — but this differs per model! E.g., A Q3=2.5 Var3/4 @80 has V: 25/31.5/40; A Q3=4 @80 H: 25/31.5/40. The full matrix table captures it. For config_level, I could scope condition by model too... complex. \n\nMaybe simpler: skip config_level and rely on matrix_tables for these multi-dim details, plus model_level for the clean per-model attributes (q3, q4, q2_over_q1). Then add type_level for common characteristics:\n- temperature_range_cold: {min: 0.1, max: 30} °C\n- temperature_range_hot: {min: 30, max: 90} °C\n- accuracy_class_cold_upper_zone: \"±2%\"... hmm. Let me define:\n  - accuracy_class: maybe type_level with value string \"Class 2\"? Not stated. I'll express MPEs:\n    - maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone_cold: value 2, unit \"%\", with reference \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4\". Could use characteristic names: mpe_upper_zone... Let me do:\n    - \"maximum_permitted_error_cold_q2_to_q4\": {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n    - \"maximum_permitted_error_cold_q1_to_q2\": {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n    - \"maximum_permitted_error_hot_q2_to_q4\": {value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n    - \"maximum_permitted_error_hot_q1_to_q2\": {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n  Hmm, that's verbose but accurate. Alternatively accuracy_class entries in matrix table only. I'll include both: matrix table preserves raw, and type_level has these.\n\n- temperature_classes: [\"T30\",\"T50\",\"T70\",\"T90\"] — as value string \"T30 / T50 / T70 / T90\".\n- pressure_range: {min: 0.3, max: 16} bar; also MPa: 0.03–1.6 MPa. Split: pressure_range: {min:0.3,max:16} bar; pressure_range_mpa: {min:0.03,max:1.6} MPa. Or one with unit bar and mention. I'll include both as separate characteristics or one as \"map\" — schema wants single structured value. I'll do pressure_range (bar) and note MPa in matrix table only. Actually maybe pressure_range: {min:0.3,max:16} bar and pressure_loss_class: {min:null, max:0.63} bar.\n- environmental_class: \"B, C\".\n\nAlso indicating device variations: Variation 1/2/3/4 — components? They're register variants. Could be components with role \"indicating device\" type_designations [\"Variation 1\",\"Variation 2\",\"Variation 3\",\"Variation 4\"]. Hmm, they're variations of the same component. I'll add a component: role \"indicating_device\", type_designations [\"Variation 1\", \"Variation 2\", \"Variation 3\", \"Variation 4\"].\n\nFootnotes:\n1) Alternatively: plug-type connection with O-ring seal\n2) Alternatively: G ¾ B and cone with body length 85mm\n\nThese footnotes attach to connection types M22x1.5(1)2) and G 1 B(1).\n\nNow, test_reports: 32 entries. role: \"test_report\". Dates: none given. Pages given. I'll list id and pages.\n\nWait — should role be \"type_evaluation_report\"? The certificate says \"associated Test Reports\". I'll use \"test_report\" for all.\n\nrevision_history: none — omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\n- address_lines: [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Dr. Gudrun Wendt\"\n- person_title: \"Head of Department\" — also Dr. R. Schwartz \"Head of Division\" signed as CIML Member. person_responsible field is singular; I'll use Dr. Gudrun Wendt, title \"Head of Department\". \n- oiml_issuer_id: not given — omit.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2006-DE1-12.05\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: \"R3-0033\"\n- page_total: 4\n- member_state: \"Germany\"\n- date_issued: \"2012-09-06\"? The two signature dates. Which one is issue date? PTB certificates typically signed by both; the later date is likely issuance. I'll use \"2012-09-07\". Hmm. Honestly ambiguous. The certificate is signed by Head of Department on 06.09.2012 and Head of Division (CIML member) on 07.09.2012. I'll go with \"2012-09-06\" as issuing authority signature... Actually, let me pick the last: 2012-09-07 when fully signed. I'll use \"2012-09-07\". Hmm, hard call. Many extractors take the date next to the issuing authority. \"The Issuing Authority — Dr. G.Wendt, Head of Department, 06.09.2012\". So the Issuing Authority signed 06.09.2012. date_issued: \"2012-09-06\". I'll go with that.\n\nNow models granularity. Let me reconsider: maybe models at (type, Q3, DN, indicating-device-variation-group, body-length)? That's the fully enumerated variant set:\n\nMinomess A:\n1. Q3=1.6, DN15, L=80, Var1/2: conn G¾B, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾\n2. Q3=1.6, DN15, L≥110, Var1/2: conn G¾B, G⅞B, G1B¹⁾, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾\n3. Q3=2.5, DN15, L=80, Var1/2: conn G¾B, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾\n4. Q3=2.5, DN20, L≥110, Var1/2: conn G1B\n5. Q3=2.5, DN15, L=80, Var3/4: conn G¾B, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾\n6. Q3=2.5, DN15, L≥110, Var3/4: conn G¾B, G⅞B, G1B¹⁾, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾\n7. Q3=4, DN20, L=80, Var3/4: conn M22x1.5\n8. Q3=4, DN20, L≥115, Var3/4: conn G1B\n\nMinomess B:\n9. Q3=1.6, DN15, L=80, Var1/2: conn G¾B\n10. Q3=1.6, DN15, L≥110, Var1/2: conn G¾B, RP½B, RP¾B\n11. Q3=2.5, DN20, L=80, Var1/2: conn G¾B\n12. Q3=2.5, DN20, L≥110, Var1/2: conn G1B, RP¾B\n\n12 models. Then model_level characteristics per model: q3, q4, dn, body_length, indicating_device. And config_level for ratio/orientation/connection per model? The ratio varies per model too (H ratio 25–80 vs 25–40). \n\nThis would be very detailed but faithful. That might be the best per rule 4 (\"MOST IMPORTANT\"). But it also risks bloat. Given the instruction emphasis on matrix detection, I'll do 12 models with attributes, plus model_level entries for q3, q4, dn, body_length. Ratios/connections go in matrix_tables.\n\nHmm, but body length has \"≥110\" — structured value {min:110, max:null} mm. Model ids: let me name them like \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L80\" etc.\n\nModel IDs:\n- \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L=80\"\n- \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L≥110\"\n- \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L=80 Var1/2\" — wait, models 3 and 5 differ only by indicating device variation. IDs: \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L=80 (Var 1/2)\" and \"(Var 3/4)\".\n\nLet me define IDs:\nA: \n1. \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L80\"\n2. \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L>=110\"\n3. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L80 Var1-2\"\n4. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN20 L>=110 Var1-2\"\n5. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L80 Var3-4\"\n6. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L>=110 Var3-4\"\n7. \"Minomess A Q3=4 DN20 L80\"\n8. \"Minomess A Q3=4 DN20 L>=115\"\nB:\n9. \"Minomess B Q3=1.6 DN15 L80\"\n10. \"Minomess B Q3=1.6 DN15 L>=110\"\n11. \"Minomess B Q3=2.5 DN20 L80\"\n12. \"Minomess B Q3=2.5 DN20 L>=110\"\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- q3 (m³/h): 1.6,1.6,2.5,2.5,2.5,2.5,4,4,1.6,1.6,2.5,2.5\n- q4 (m³/h): 2,2,3.125×4,5,5,2,2,3.125,3.125 — note B Q4 \"2,0\" → 2.0\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 all\n- nominal_diameter_dn: 15,15,15,20,15,15,20,20,15,15,20,20\n- body_length (mm): 80 or {min:110,max:null} / 115\n- indicating_device: \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\" or \"Variation 3 / Variation 4\"\n\nThen config_level for the orientation ratio? Maybe:\n- attribute \"q3_over_q1_ratio\", axis \"orientation\", values with condition strings including model context... e.g. {condition: \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L80, H\", value: \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\"}. That's basically duplicating matrix tables. I'll keep the ratios in matrix_tables only, since matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables \"that don't fit elsewhere\". And connection types per model also in matrix_tables. But wait — rule 4 says populate model_level from matrix; the key varying numeric attributes (q3, q4) are covered. Good.\n\nActually, I could add model_level for \"q3_over_q1_ratio_horizontal\" as a string per model... The ratio sets: H varies: 25–80 (many), 25/31.5/40 (A Q3=2.5 DN15 L80 Var3/4; A Q3=4 L80). V varies: 25 (most), 25/31.5/40 (A Q3=2.5 Var3/4 both; A Q3=4). I'll add these as model_level string values to be thorough:\n\nmodel_level:\n- q3_over_q1_ratio_horizontal (unit null, string values)\n- q3_over_q1_ratio_vertical\n\nHmm, strings of lists... acceptable as string values. I'll include them — they're meaningful metrological ratios.\n\nAlso connection_type per model as string values in model_level? That might overload. Matrix tables have it. I'll include connection types in matrix_tables rows and in components? No — components are indicating device only. Let me include connection_type as a model_level string too? It's a list per model (\"G ¾ B / M22x1.5\"). I'll keep in matrix_tables only to avoid overload. Hmm, but then components/config... fine.\n\nMatrix tables construction:\n\nTable A1 (Q3=1.6): columns: attribute, then 2 value columns. Rows:\n- q3 (m³/h): 1.6 (both)\n- q4 (m³/h): 2\n- q2/q1: 1.6\n- body_length_mm: 80 / ≥110\n- nominal_diameter: DN 15\n- indicating_device: Variation 1 / Variation 2\n- q3/q1 orientation H: 25 / 31,5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\n- q3/q1 orientation V: 25\n- connection_type: [G ¾ B, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾] / [G ¾ B, G ⅞ B, G 1 B¹⁾, M22x1.5¹⁾²⁾]\n\nFor matrix_tables, I'll use rows as objects keyed by config column: e.g. rows: [{\"body_length_mm\": 80, \"nominal_diameter\": \"DN 15\", ...}, {...}]. Restructure tables into per-variant rows? The original layout is attribute-rows × config-columns. For preservation, I could keep the original orientation: rows keyed by attribute with columns \"config_1\", \"config_2\". Let me do:\n\n{\n \"name\": \"Minomess A – Q3 = 1.6 m³/h type details\",\n \"columns\": [\"attribute\", \"body_length_80_mm\", \"body_length_ge_110_mm\"],\n \"rows\": [\n   {\"attribute\": \"q3_m3_h\", \"body_length_80_mm\": 1.6, \"body_length_ge_110_mm\": 1.6},\n   ...\n ]\n}\n\nHmm, the example in the instructions used columns as attributes and rows as models. For consistency with the R137 example, transpose: columns = attributes, rows = variants. Let me do that — cleaner:\n\nTable \"Minomess A — Q3 = 1.6 m³/h\":\ncolumns: [\"variant\", \"q3_m3_h\", \"q4_m3_h\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"body_length_mm\", \"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"indicating_device\", \"q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_h\", \"q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_v\", \"connection_type\"]\nrows:\n- {\"variant\": \"L=80\", \"q3_m3_h\": 1.6, \"q4_m3_h\": 2, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"body_length_mm\": 80, \"nominal_diameter_dn\": \"DN 15\", \"indicating_device\": \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\", \"q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_h\": \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\", \"q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_v\": \"25\", \"connection_type\": \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5 (1)(2)\"}\n- {\"variant\": \"L≥110\", ..., \"body_length_mm\": \"≥110\", connection: \"G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B (1); M22x1.5 (1)(2)\"}\n\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", null, null, null, null, null]\n\nSimilarly for other tables. Good, consistent with R137 example.\n\nTable \"Minomess A — Q3 = 2.5 m³/h\": 4 variants:\n- Var1/2 DN15 L80: conn G ¾ B; M22x1.5 (1)(2); H ratios 25–80; V 25\n- Var1/2 DN20 L≥110: conn G 1 B; H 25–80; V 25\n- Var3/4 DN15 L80: conn G ¾ B; M22x1.5 (1)(2); H 25/31.5/40; V 25/31.5/40\n- Var3/4 DN15 L≥110: conn G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B (1); M22x1.5 (1)(2); H 25–80; V 25/31.5/40\n\nTable \"Minomess A — Q3 = 4 m³/h\": 2 variants:\n- L80: DN20, Var3/4, H 25/31.5/40, V 25/31.5/40, conn M22x1.5\n- L≥115: DN20, Var3/4, H 25–80, V 25/31.5/40, conn G 1 B\n\nTable \"Minomess B — Q3 = 1.6 and 2.5 m³/h\": 4 variants:\n- Q3=1.6 DN15 L80: q4 2.0, Var1/2, H 25–80, V 25, conn G ¾ B\n- Q3=1.6 DN15 L≥110: conn G ¾ B; RP ½ B; RP ¾ B\n- Q3=2.5 DN20 L80: q4 3.125, conn G ¾ B\n- Q3=2.5 DN20 L≥110: conn G 1 B; RP ¾ B\n\nTable \"Common metrological characteristics\":\nrows per attribute: temperature_range 0.1–30°C / 30–90°C; accuracy; temp classes; pressure range; pressure-loss class; environmental class.\n\nNow characteristics.type_level (whole-cert):\n- temperature_range_cold_water: {min:0.1, max:30}, \"°C\"\n- temperature_range_hot_water: {min:30, max:90}, \"°C\"\n- maximum_permitted_error_upper_zone (Q2≤Q≤Q4) cold: 2%... Let me name:\n  - \"maximum_permitted_error_cold_q2_to_q4\": {value: 2, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n  - \"maximum_permitted_error_cold_q1_to_q2\": {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n  - \"maximum_permitted_error_hot_q2_to_q4\": {value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n  - \"maximum_permitted_error_hot_q1_to_q2\": {value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n- temperature_class: \"T30 / T50 / T70 / T90\" (string)\n- pressure_range: {min:0.3, max:16}, \"bar\" — and maybe \"pressure_range_mpa\": {min:0.03,max:1.6}, \"MPa\"\n- pressure_loss_class: {max: 0.63} → {min:null, max:0.63}, \"bar\"\n- environmental_class: \"B, C\"\n- q2_over_q1: 1.6 — actually this is same for all models; could be type_level. But it's in model tables... it's constant. Put in type_level: \"q2_over_q1_ratio\": 1.6. Also in model_level? Redundant. I'll put q2_over_q1 in type_level only since constant. Hmm, but model_level is where per-model matrices go; constant values go type_level. Good.\n- indicating_device_variations: \"Variation 1 / Variation 2 / Variation 3 / Variation 4\"? That's covered by models. Skip.\n\nmodel_level entries (12 models each):\n1. q3 (m³/h): values per model: 1.6, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 2.5, 2.5, 4, 4, 1.6, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5\n2. q4 (m³/h): 2, 2, 3.125, 3.125, 3.125, 3.125, 5, 5, 2.0→2, 2, 3.125, 3.125\n3. nominal_diameter_dn: \"DN 15\" or 15? The table says \"DN 15\"/\"DN 20\" for A, \"15\"/\"20\" for B. Value: 15/20 numeric with unit null? DN is dimensionless designation. I'll use numeric 15/20.\n4. body_length_mm: 80 or {min:110, max:null}; last: {min:115, max:null}\n5. indicating_device: \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\" or \"Variation 3 / Variation 4\"\n6. q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_h: string sets\n7. q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_v: string sets\n\nDecimal conversion: German commas: 1,6 → 1.6; 2,5 → 2.5; 3,125 → 3.125; 0,1 → 0.1; 0,3 → 0.3; 0,63 → 0.63; 31,5 → 31.5; 1,6 → 1.6.\n\nFootnote markers on connection strings: mark \"(1)\" \"(2)\". In matrix table strings I'll write \"G 1 B (1)\" etc. And footnotes array:\n- {marker: \"1)\", text: \"Alternatively: plug-type connection with O-ring seal\"}\n- {marker: \"2)\", text: \"Alternatively: G ¾ B and cone with body length 85 mm\"}\n\nTest reports (32). Let me list them with roles. ETK = cold (Kalt), ETW = warm. All role \"test_report\". Pages as given.\n\n1. ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 41\n2. ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 53\n3. ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 41\n4. ETW Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 53\n5. ETK Q3-4 V4 80 — 61\n6. ETW Q3-4 V4 80 — 89\n7. ETK Q3-4 V3 115 — 61\n8. ETW Q3-4 V3 115 — 89\n9. ETK Q3-1,6 V1 110 — 40\n10. ETW Q3-1,6 V1 110 — 52\n11. ETK Q3-4 V3 CC 115 — 53\n12. ETW Q3-4 V3 CC 115 — 77\n13. ETK Q3-4 V4 130 — 61\n14. ETW Q3-4 V4 130 — 89\n15. ETK Q3-4 V3 8R 115 — 61\n16. ETW Q3-4 V3 8R 115 — 89\n17. ETK Q3-4 V3 KU 130 — 61\n18. ETW Q3-4 V3 KU 130 — 89\n19. ETK Q3-2,5 V3 110 — 161\n20. ETW Q3-2,5 V3 110 — 227\n21. ETK Q3-2,5 V4 80 — 145\n22. ETW Q3-2,5 V4 80 — 245\n23. ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 161\n24. ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 — 277\n25. ETK Q3-2,5 V3 80 — 145\n26. ETW Q3-2,5 V3 80 — 245\n27. ETK Q3-2,5 V3 CC 110 — 57\n28. ETW Q3-2,5 V3 CC 110 — 85\n29. ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 59\n30. ETW Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 — 87\n\nThat's 30 entries (15 rows × 2). Let me recount the table rows: \nRow1: ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 (41) | ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 (53)\nRow2: ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 (41) | ETW Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 (53)\nRow3: ETK Q3-4 V4 80 (61) | ETW Q3-4 V4 80 (89)\nRow4: ETK Q3-4 V3 115 (61) | ETW Q3-4 V3 115 (89)\nRow5: ETK Q3-1,6 V1 110 (40) | ETW Q3-1,6 V1 110 (52)\nRow6: ETK Q3-4 V3 CC 115 (53) | ETW Q3-4 V3 CC 115 (77)\nRow7: ETK Q3-4 V4 130 (61) | ETW Q3-4 V4 130 (89)\nRow8: ETK Q3-4 V3 8R 115 (61) | ETW Q3-4 V3 8R 115 (89)\nRow9: ETK Q3-4 V3 KU 130 (61) | ETW Q3-4 V3 KU 130 (89)\nRow10: ETK Q3-2,5 V3 110 (161) | ETW Q3-2,5 V3 110 (227)\nRow11: ETK Q3-2,5 V4 80 (145) | ETW Q3-2,5 V4 80 (245)\nRow12: ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 (161) | ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 (277)\nRow13: ETK Q3-2,5 V3 80 (145) | ETW Q3-2,5 V3 80 (245)\nRow14: ETK Q3-2,5 V3 CC 110 (57) | ETW Q3-2,5 V3 CC 110 (85)\nRow15: ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 (59) | ETW Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110 (87)\n\n30 test reports.\n\nComponents: indicating device variations 1–4 with photo descriptions. I'll add components entry: role \"indicating_device\", type_designations [\"Variation 1\", \"Variation 2\", \"Variation 3\", \"Variation 4\"]. Should alternatives be \"OR\"? The variations are alternatives per model. I could set alternatives: \"OR\". Reasonable.\n\nRecommendation: \n{\n \"id\": \"R49\",\n \"edition\": 2006,\n \"scheme\": \"A\",\n ... accuracy_classes omitted\n}\nMaybe note parts R49-1/-2/-3 — schema has no field; I could omit or add. I'll keep id \"R49\", edition 2006.\n\nCategory: \"Water meter\".\n\ncertified_type.type_designations: [\"Minomess A\", \"Minomess B\"].\nmodule_designation: null.\ndescription: \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water; single jet meter with mechanical register.\"\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name, address_lines: [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Dr. Gudrun Wendt\"\n- person_title: \"Head of Department\"\n- website: null / omit.\n\nAlso CIML member Dr. R. Schwartz, Head of Division — no schema field; could add as another person? The schema for issuing_authority has single person fields. I might add \"person_responsible\": \"Dr. Gudrun Wendt\" and skip Schwartz, or add him in revision/notes. I'll skip extra fields to conform. Hmm, maybe include both? Schema strictness — I'll keep one and perhaps add \"ciml_member\" — not in schema. Rules say conform to schema; omit extra fields (don't fabricate structure). I'll omit Schwartz. Actually... it's real info. But schema conformance matters more. Omit.\n\ndate_issued: \"2012-09-06\". Let me reconsider once more: The layout shows \"The Issuing Authority — Dr. G.Wendt, Head of Department, 06.09.2012\" and \"The CIML Member — Dr. R. Schwartz, Head of Division, 07.09.2012\". The certificate issue date = date signed by issuing authority = 06.09.2012 → \"2012-09-06\". Go with that.\n\nNow, model ids — I'll use readable ids:\nA: \n1. \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L=80\"\n2. \"Minomess A Q3=1.6 DN15 L>=110\"\n3. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L=80 (Var 1/2)\"\n4. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN20 L>=110 (Var 1/2)\"\n5. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L=80 (Var 3/4)\"\n6. \"Minomess A Q3=2.5 DN15 L>=110 (Var 3/4)\"\n7. \"Minomess A Q3=4 DN20 L=80\"\n8. \"Minomess A Q3=4 DN20 L>=115\"\nB:\n9. \"Minomess B Q3=1.6 DN15 L=80\"\n10. \"Minomess B Q3=1.6 DN15 L>=110\"\n11. \"Minomess B Q3=2.5 DN20 L=80\"\n12. \"Minomess B Q3=2.5 DN20 L>=110\"\n\nFor models 1 & 2 the indicating device is Var 1/2 (only). Models 7 & 8: Var 3/4.\n\nmodel_level values arrays must use these model ids.\n\nLet me also include \"connection_types\" per model? I'll include as model_level string attribute \"connection_type\" with combined strings — actually this adds value since connections are key config. Values like \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5 (1)(2)\". Let me include it — it's informative and traceable. Strings with footnote markers though! Rule 1 says never include footnote markers in labels; footnote markers in values should be represented via footnote_markers array. For connection strings, the value is text; I'll strip markers and add footnote_markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"] on those values. E.g. model 1 connection: value \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5\", footnote_markers: [\"1)\",\"2)\"]. Model 2: \"G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B; M22x1.5\", footnote_markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"] — note G 1 B has (1) and M22x1.5 has (1)(2). Combined markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"]. OK.\n\nConnection strings use ¾, ⅞, ½ symbols. Keep unicode.\n\nFor model_level, structure: {\"attribute\": \"connection_type\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [{\"model\": ..., \"value\": \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\",\"2)\"]}, ...]}.\n\nHmm wait — the structured value rule says every value must be object with value/unit_symbol/footnote_markers. For model_level, the schema shows values: {model, value, footnote_markers[]} — unit_symbol at attribute level. OK.\n\nAlso ratios: \"q3_over_q1_ratio_horizontal\": values e.g. \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\". Some models have shorter set:\n- M1 (A 1.6 L80): H 25–80 set; V \"25\"\n- M2: same H; V \"25\"\n- M3 (A 2.5 DN15 L80 Var1/2): H 25–80; V 25\n- M4 (A 2.5 DN20 ≥110 Var1/2): H 25–80; V 25\n- M5 (A 2.5 DN15 L80 Var3/4): H \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"; V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\n- M6 (A 2.5 DN15 ≥110 Var3/4): H 25–80; V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\n- M7 (A 4 L80): H \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"; V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\n- M8 (A 4 ≥115): H 25–80; V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\n- M9 (B 1.6 L80): H 25–80; V \"25\"\n- M10: H 25–80; V \"25\"\n- M11 (B 2.5 L80): H 25–80; V \"25\"\n- M12: H 25–80; V \"25\"\n\nNow let me also double check Table 2 column assignment. Table 2 header: \"Minomess A\" colspan 4. Rows:\n- Q3 = 2.5 (colspan 4)\n- Q4 = 3.125 (colspan 4)\n- Q2/Q1 = 1.6\n- Body length: 80 | ≥110 | 80 | ≥110\n- DN: DN 15 | DN 20 | DN 15 (colspan 2)\n- Indicating device: Variation 1 / Variation 2 (colspan 2) | Variation 3 / Variation 4 (colspan 2)\n- Q3/Q1 H: 25/31.5/40/50/63/80 (colspan 2) | 25/31.5/40 | 25/31.5/40/50/63/80\n- Q3/Q1 V: 25 (colspan 2) | 25/31.5/40 (colspan 2)\n- Connection type: [G ¾ B, M22x1.5(1)2)] | [G 1 B] | [G ¾ B, M22x1.5(1)2)] | [G ¾ B, G ⅞ B, G 1B(1), M22x1.5(1)2)]\n\nSo col1: Var1/2, DN15, L80; col2: Var1/2, DN20, L≥110; col3: Var3/4, DN15, L80; col4: Var3/4, DN15, L≥110. Matches my earlier reading. \n\nTable 3 (Q3=4): Q3 4, Q4 5, Q2/Q1 1.6, body 80 | ≥115, DN 20 (colspan2), Var 3/4, H: 25/31.5/40 | 25–80, V: 25/31.5/40 (colspan 2), conn: M22x1.5 | G 1 B. Good.\n\nTable 4 (Minomess B): Q3: 1.6 (colspan2) | 2.5 (colspan2); Q4: 2,0 | 3,125; Q2/Q1 1.6 (colspan4); body: 80 | ≥110 | 80 | ≥110; DN: 15 (colspan2) | 20 (colspan2); Var 1/2 (colspan4); H: 25–80 (colspan4); V: 25 (colspan4); conn: G¾B | G¾B, RP½B, RP¾B | G¾B | G1B, RP¾B. Good.\n\nWait — table 4 row \"Nominal diameter (DN)\": <td colspan=\"2\">15</td><td colspan=\"2\">20</td>. Yes.\n\nHmm interesting: Minomess B Q3=2.5 has DN 20 (vs A's DN15/DN20 mix). OK.\n\nNow the photos: ignore (rule 11 mentions image markers; photo descriptions — ignore). The dial text mentions \"R80\", \"ETIK-M\" etc. Ignore.\n\nDescription: \"Single jet meter with mechanical register\" — include.\n\nNow build JSON.\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-DE1-12.05\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"project_number\": \"R3-0033\",\n    \"page_total\": 4,\n    \"member_state\": \"Germany\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2012-09-06\",\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": null\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Bundesallee 100\", \"38116 Braunschweig\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Dr. Gudrun Wendt\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head of Department\",\n    \"phone\": null,\n    \"email\": null,\n    \"website\": null,\n    \"oiml_issuer_id\": null\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\"name\": \"Zenner International GmbH & Co. KG\", \"address_lines\": [\"Römerstadt 4\", \"66121 Saarbrücken\", \"Germany\"]}\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [ six entries ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Minomess A\", \"Minomess B\"],\n    \"module_designation\": null,\n    \"description\": \"Water meter intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water; single jet meter with mechanical register.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"Minomess\",\n    \"models\": [12 entries with attributes]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\"role\": \"indicating_device\", \"type_designations\": [\"Variation 1\", \"Variation 2\", \"Variation 3\", \"Variation 4\"], \"alternatives\": \"OR\"}\n  ],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [...],\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [...],\n  \"recommendation\": {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2006, \"scheme\": \"A\"},\n  \"test_reports\": [30],\n  \"footnotes\": [2]\n}\n\nFor models' attributes: {\"q3_m3_h\": 1.6, \"q4_m3_h\": 2, \"nominal_diameter_dn\": 15, \"body_length_mm\": 80, \"indicating_device\": \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\"} etc. body_length for ≥110: {\"min\": 110, \"max\": null}? attributes{} is free-form; I'll write value as \"≥110\" string or {\"min\":110,\"max\":null}. I'll use string \">= 110\"? Better keep consistent: {\"min\": 110, \"max\": null}. Since attributes{} is free-form, an object is fine.\n\nHmm wait — for model attributes, simpler flat values might be better: \"body_length_mm\": \"≥110\". I'll use the range object to be consistent with structured-value rules.\n\nmodel_level arrays: attributes q3, q4, q2_over_q1(constant → skip, it's in type_level), nominal_diameter_dn, body_length, indicating_device, ratio H, ratio V, connection_type.\n\nThat's 8 model_level entries × 12 values. That's fine.\n\nLet me also decide whether q2_over_q1 should be type_level: \"q2_over_q1_ratio\": {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: null}. Yes.\n\ntype_level list:\n- q2_over_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- temperature_range_cold_water: {min:0.1,max:30} °C\n- temperature_range_hot_water: {min:30,max:90} °C\n- maximum_permitted_error_cold_q2_to_q4: 2 %\n- maximum_permitted_error_cold_q1_to_q2: 5 %\n- maximum_permitted_error_hot_q2_to_q4: 3 %\n- maximum_permitted_error_hot_q1_to_q2: 5 %\n- temperature_class: \"T30 / T50 / T70 / T90\"\n- pressure_range: {min:0.3,max:16} bar\n- pressure_range_alternate: {min:0.03,max:1.6} MPa — name \"pressure_range_megapascal\"? I'll name \"pressure_range_mpa\" unit \"MPa\".\n- pressure_loss_class: {min:null, max:0.63} bar — it's a class boundary: max pressure loss ≤0.63 bar. Value {\"max\":0.63} with min null. Or as class designation string \"0.63 bar (0.063 MPa)\". I'll use {min:null,max:0.63} bar and add \"pressure_loss_class_mpa\": {\"max\":0.063} MPa? Might be overkill; the matrix table captures raw. I'll include just bar version; and MPa in matrix table.\n- environmental_class: \"B, C\"\n\nStructured value objects in type_level: shape {value, unit_symbol, unit_id, footnote_markers}. E.g.:\n\"q2_over_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null, \"footnote_markers\": []}\n\nNow matrix_tables. I'll create 5 tables. Define columns and rows per R137 style.\n\nTable 1: name \"Minomess A – Q3 = 1.6 m³/h\"\ncolumns: [\"variant\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"body_length\", \"nominal_diameter\", \"indicating_device\", \"q3_over_q1_orientation_h\", \"q3_over_q1_orientation_v\", \"connection_type\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"mm\", null, null, null, null, null]\nrows:\n[\n {\"variant\": \"Length 80 mm\", \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"body_length\": 80, \"nominal_diameter\": \"DN 15\", \"indicating_device\": \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\", \"q3_over_q1_orientation_h\": \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\", \"q3_over_q1_orientation_v\": \"25\", \"connection_type\": \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5\"},\n {\"variant\": \"Length ≥ 110 mm\", \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"body_length\": \"≥110\", \"nominal_diameter\": \"DN 15\", \"indicating_device\": \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\", \"q3_over_q1_orientation_h\": \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\", \"q3_over_q1_orientation_v\": \"25\", \"connection_type\": \"G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B; M22x1.5\"}\n]\nfootnotes: [\"1)\", \"2)\"] — description: which values carry footnotes: M22x1.5 (1)(2), G 1 B (1). I'll append markers in the connection strings here? Rule 1 says never include footnote markers — that's for labels. For matrix cell content, better to keep clean and list footnotes at table level. I'll add footnote markers in a footnote_markers subfield? Rows are plain objects. I'll append footnote refs like \"M22x1.5 (1)(2)\"? The instruction rule 1 targets canonical labels. For matrix tables, preserving raw content including markers is arguably fine and traceable. But to be safe, I'll keep cell text clean and note in description: \"Connection types M22x1.5 marked (1)(2); G 1 B marked (1) — see certificate footnotes.\" Hmm, that loses precision. Alternatively keep markers in cell strings: \"G 1 B (1)\". I think including them in matrix cells is acceptable and faithful — the rule says labels shouldn't have LaTeX/footnote junk; matrix rows are data preservation. I'll include the markers in connection_type strings as they appear: \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5 (1)(2)\". And table footnotes list the definitions.\n\nTable 2: \"Minomess A – Q3 = 2.5 m³/h\", 4 rows as described.\n\nTable 3: \"Minomess A – Q3 = 4 m³/h\", 2 rows.\n\nTable 4: \"Minomess B – Q3 = 1.6 m³/h and 2.5 m³/h\", 4 rows.\n\nTable 5: \"Common characteristics\", columns [\"characteristic\", \"cold_water_0.1°C_to_30°C\", \"hot_water_30°C_to_90°C\"]? The accuracy row is temperature-dependent; temperature range row too. Others span both. Rows:\n- temperature_range: cold \"0.1 °C – 30 °C\", hot \"30 °C – 90 °C\"\n- accuracy_class: cold \"±2 % (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4); ±5 % (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\", hot \"±3 % (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4); ±5 % (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\"\n- temperature_classes: \"T30 / T50 / T70 / T90\" (both)\n- pressure_range: \"0.3 bar (0.03 MPa) – 16 bar (1.6 MPa)\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"0.63 bar (0.063 MPa)\"\n- environmental_class: \"B, C\"\n\nAlternatively make table 5 rows as objects keyed by characteristic. I'll structure: columns [\"attribute\",\"cold_water_0_1_to_30_c\",\"hot_water_30_to_90_c\"].\n\nUnicode: use \"≤\" and \"<\" fine in JSON.\n\nNow let me also double-check \"Q3/Q1 Orientation H\" values \"25 / 31,5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\" → convert commas: \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\".\n\nTest report IDs keep original (German decimal commas in IDs like \"Q3-2,5\" — IDs should stay verbatim). Yes, keep verbatim: \"ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110\".\n\nPages counts numeric.\n\nShould I include role \"test_report\" for all. Yes.\n\nAlso maybe include a note that ETK/ETW correspond to cold/hot. Not in schema. Could set role: ETK → \"test_report\", fine.\n\nNow assemble the 12 model attribute objects:\n\nM1: {\"q3_m3_h\": 1.6, \"q4_m3_h\": 2, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6, \"nominal_diameter_dn\": \"DN 15\", \"body_length_mm\": 80, \"indicating_device\": \"Variation 1 / Variation 2\"}\nM2: same but body {\"min\":110,\"max\":null}\nM3: q3 2.5, q4 3.125, DN 15, L 80, Var 1/2\nM4: q3 2.5, q4 3.125, DN 20, L ≥110, Var 1/2\nM5: q3 2.5, q4 3.125, DN 15, L 80, Var 3/4\nM6: q3 2.5, q4 3.125, DN 15, L ≥110, Var 3/4\nM7: q3 4, q4 5, DN 20, L 80, Var 3/4\nM8: q3 4, q4 5, DN 20, L ≥115 (min 115), Var 3/4\nM9: q3 1.6, q4 2.0, DN \"15\", L 80, Var 1/2\nM10: q3 1.6, q4 2.0, DN 15, L ≥110, Var 1/2\nM11: q3 2.5, q4 3.125, DN 20, L 80, Var 1/2\nM12: q3 2.5, q4 3.125, DN 20, L ≥110, Var 1/2\n\nDN numeric 15/20. For B, the table says \"15\"/\"20\" (no DN prefix). Use 15, 20 numbers.\n\nbody_length as value: for M2 etc use {\"min\": 110, \"max\": null}. In attributes{} free-form — okay.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n\n1. {\"attribute\": \"q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...12...]}\n2. {\"attribute\": \"q4\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [...]}\n3. {\"attribute\": \"nominal_diameter_dn\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [...]} values 15/20 numbers... maybe \"DN 15\" strings? Numeric is cleaner: 15.\n4. {\"attribute\": \"body_length\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"values\": [80 or {\"min\":110,\"max\":null}]}\n5. {\"attribute\": \"indicating_device\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [\"Variation 1 / Variation 2\" | \"Variation 3 / Variation 4\"]}\n6. {\"attribute\": \"q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_h\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [strings]}\n7. {\"attribute\": \"q3_over_q1_ratio_orientation_v\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [strings]}\n8. {\"attribute\": \"connection_type\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"values\": [strings w/ footnote_markers]}\n\nConnection strings per model (clean + markers):\nM1: \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5\", markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"]\nM2: \"G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B; M22x1.5\", markers [\"1)\",\"2)\"] — note G 1 B only (1), M22x1.5 (1)(2). Represent combined [\"1)\",\"2)\"]? Slightly imprecise. Alternative: keep markers inline: \"G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B (1); M22x1.5 (1)(2)\" and no footnote_markers array. For model_level values, inline markers in strings conflict with \"never include footnote markers\"? Rule 1 says labels — \"Canonical labels... NEVER include ... footnote markers\". That's about attribute labels. Values: rule 7 says record markers in footnote_markers on that value. So better: clean string + footnote_markers array. For M2 the union is [\"1)\",\"2)\"] — acceptable.\nM3: \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5\", [\"1)\",\"2)\"]\nM4: \"G 1 B\", []\nM5: \"G ¾ B; M22x1.5\", [\"1)\",\"2)\"]\nM6: \"G ¾ B; G ⅞ B; G 1 B; M22x1.5\", [\"1)\",\"2)\"]\nM7: \"M22x1.5\", []\nM8: \"G 1 B\", []\nM9: \"G ¾ B\", []\nM10: \"G ¾ B; RP ½ B; RP ¾ B\", []\nM11: \"G ¾ B\", []\nM12: \"G 1 B; RP ¾ B\", []\n\nRatio H strings:\nStandard: \"25 / 31.5 / 40 / 50 / 63 / 80\"\nShort: \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\nRatio V:\n\"25\" or \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\n\nAssignments:\nM1: H std, V \"25\"\nM2: H std, V \"25\"\nM3: H std, V \"25\"\nM4: H std, V \"25\"\nM5: H short, V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\nM6: H std, V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\nM7: H short, V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\nM8: H std, V \"25 / 31.5 / 40\"\nM9: H std, V \"25\"\nM10: H std, V \"25\"\nM11: H std, V \"25\"\nM12: H std, V \"25\"\n\nCould convert ratio strings to ranges {min:25, max:80}? The sets are the R-ratio options; \"R80\" on dial. String preserves full set. Keep strings.\n\nTest reports list — 30 objects:\n{\"id\": \"ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110\", \"pages\": 41, \"role\": \"test_report\"} etc. Schema fields: id, date, pages, role. date omitted.\n\nWait: duplicates exist — \"ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110\" appears in row 1 (41 pages) and row 12 (161 pages). Same id, different page counts! Row 1: \"ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 that includes 41 pages\"; row 12: \"ETK Q3-2,5 V4 110 that includes 161 pages\". That's odd but that's what the document says. Possibly OCR duplication of similar report versions. I'll preserve both entries as given. Same for ETW Q3-2,5 V4 110 (53 and 277). And \"ETK Q3-2,5 V3 KU 110\" (41, row2) and (59, row15); ETW ... KU 110 (53, 87). So the table seems to list older short reports and newer comprehensive ones. Preserve as-is — 30 entries total. JSON allows duplicate ids in array.\n\nFootnotes:\n{\"marker\": \"1)\", \"text\": \"Alternatively: plug-type connection with O-ring seal\"}\n{\"marker\": \"2)\", \"text\": \"Alternatively: G ¾ B and cone with body length 85 mm\"}\n\nRecommendation: parts R49-1, R49-2, R49-3 all Edition 2006. I'll set {\"id\": \"R49\", \"edition\": 2006, \"scheme\": \"A\"}. accuracy_classes: skip (not explicitly labeled). Hmm — actually, could I include \"accuracy_classes\": [\"2\"]? The accuracy class table implies class 2 for cold (±2/±5). For hot ±3/±5 — in OIML R49-1:2006, accuracy classes are 1 and 2; class 2 MPE: Q2≤Q≤Q4: ±2% for T30; for T50/T70/T90 hot meters the upper MPE is ±3%? Let me recall R49-1:2006 §5.2: \"Maximum permissible errors... in the lower zone: class 1 ±3%, class 2 ±5%; upper zone: class 1 ±1%, class 2 ±2%. For water meters intended for hot water above 30 °C, the MPE in the upper zone is increased to ±3% for class 2\"? I genuinely recall ISO 4064:1993 / R49 combining to give ±3% for hot class 2. So all these meters are accuracy class 2. I'm fairly confident, but rule 8 says don't fabricate; deriving class 2 is inference. I'll omit accuracy_classes to be safe. Actually hmm, including it could be considered inference from the MPE table. I'll omit — matrix table preserves the MPE data.\n\nScheme A vs B: R49/2006-DE1-12.05 — old OIML certificates are under the OIML Certificate System (Scheme A). Scheme B was introduced 2018+ for specific RRs. So \"A\".\n\ncertificate.oiml_issuer_id: not present — null or omit. I'll omit nulls? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll include only present fields; but including nulls is allowed by schema? \"Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" So nulls OK. I'll use null sparingly.\n\nproject_number \"R3-0033\" — that's PTB's internal reference printed on page 1. Reasonable to map to project_number. I'll include it.\n\nLet me now also double check the applicant address: \"Römerstadt 4, 66121 Saarbrücken\". Fine.\n\nManufacturers address lines — keep as in doc, decode &amp; to &.\n\n1. ZENNER International GmbH & Co KG — Mittelstraße 42, 09619 Mulda, GERMANY\n2. ZENNER International GmbH & Co KG — Römerstadt 4, 66121 Saarbrücken, GERMANY\n3. ZENNER do Brasil Instrumentos de Med